  "response": "Competing explanations of an abrupt score jump are adjudicated not by weighing their plausibility in the abstract, but by deriving from each a distinct set of observable consequences and testing the forensic record against them. Genuine instructional reform predicts gains distributed across items roughly in proportion to item difficulty, visible in classroom work, and sustained in later cohorts; irregular administration predicts signatures in the answer documents themselves. Lincoln Elementary's fifth-grade math proficiency rate rose from 38 percent to 81 percent in a single year, and that forty-three-point jump is precisely the evidence that showed the state testing agency it was dealing with a statistical outlier, which is how the agency flagged the school's one-year score gain. That outlier designation led the state to release anonymized answer-sheet and item-response data to the research team.\n\nErasure analysis measures the rate of wrong-to-right answer changes on scanned answer sheets. Lincoln Elementary's wrong-to-right erasure rate exceeded the state mean by more than five standard deviations, whereas on legitimately completed answer sheets right-to-wrong erasures outnumber wrong-to-right erasures. Erasure analysis identifies with certainty which individual student altered each answer, a property that cannot be squared with the technique's being no more than a measure of wrong-to-right change rates across a scanned document; an aggregate rate attaches to a sheet of paper, not to a culprit.\n\nItem-level response patterns showed implausibly high accuracy on the most difficult test items, which is the trace a transcription of correct answers leaves behind rather than the profile incremental teaching gains produce. That statistical work was finished before any witness was questioned. Investigators interviewed teachers and proctors who were present during the test administration, and as a result four teachers reported that completed answer sheets were stored unsealed overnight in a school office.\n\nThe implausibly high accuracy on the hardest items indicates that proctors supplied correct answers to students during the test session. The overnight storage of completed sheets, unsealed, in a school office resulted in school staff altering student answer sheets after the test session had ended. At least one of these two mechanisms was at work at Lincoln Elementary, answers fed to pupils in the room or sheets reworked once the room was empty, and perhaps both were. After the four teachers' accounts of the unsealed sheets, either the state invalidated at least some of Lincoln Elementary's fifth-grade test scores or no test scores at Lincoln Elementary were invalidated; exactly one of those two dispositions obtained, for they cannot both stand. The adjudication therefore turns on convergence: an instructional-reform account must explain the document-level signatures as well as the headline gain, and it cannot.",
